{"name":"Fertile Soil","hex":"#8e603c","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fertile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of land, etc.: capable of growing abundant crops; productive.","Of one's imagination, etc.: active, productive, prolific."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fertile"},{"term":"soil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture of mineral particles and organic material, used to support plant growth.","The unconsolidated mineral or organic material on the immediate surface of the earth that serves as a natural medium for the growth of land plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make dirty.","To become dirty or soiled."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soil fertility","blocks":[{"text":"Soil fertility refers to the ability of soil to sustain agricultural plant growth, i.e. to provide plant habitat and result in sustained and consistent yields of high quality (see also soil health). A fertile soil has the following properties:"},{"items":["The ability to supply essential plant nutrients and water in adequate amounts and proportions for plant growth and reproduction; and","The absence of toxic substances which may inhibit plant growth e.g. Fe2+ which leads to nutrient toxicity."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Soil_Horizons.svg/330px-Soil_Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_fertility","wikidata":"Q202080"},{"title":"Soil","blocks":[{"text":"Soil, also commonly referred to as earth, is a mixture of organic matter, minerals, gases, water, and organisms that together support the life of plants and soil organisms. Some scientific definitions distinguish dirt from soil by restricting the former term specifically to displaced soil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Stagnogley.JPG/330px-Stagnogley.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil","wikidata":"Q36133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg/500px-Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg/1280px-Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg/500px-Desert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_%283%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Desert east of Birdsville, Australia. Much of Australia is sparsely populated as its desert soils are mostly infertile; thus unable to support larger scale human habitation.[1][2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADesert_East_of_Birdsville_-_panoramio_(3).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Stagnogley.JPG/500px-Stagnogley.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Stagnogley.JPG/1280px-Stagnogley.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Stagnogley.JPG/500px-Stagnogley.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface-water-gley developed in glacial till in Northern Ireland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStagnogley.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/EAgronom_4okt2023_L-1120.jpg/500px-EAgronom_4okt2023_L-1120.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&